  "query_preview": "..Upper level low that helped enhance the storm\ncoverage and intensity yesterday has tracked into southeastern\nUtah. Some warmer and drier air aloft has moved in behind this \nlow which has limited convection so far this afternoon and is\nexpected to keep storm coverage more limited this afternoon. Still\ncan't rule out the possibility of one of these storms producing\nenough rain for flash flooding, especially with numerous spots\nacross the region having saturated soil.\n\nStronger high pressure will move west into the region Thursday and\npark over the Four Corners through the weekend. This will bring\nsome drier air and warmer temperatures aloft which will start\nreducing the storm coverage with the high country having the best\nchances. Steering flow aloft starting Thursday will push any\nstorms that form over the higher terrain northward which should\nlimit some of the coverage over Yavapai and northern Gila\nCounties. By Friday and Saturday temperatures will warm above \naverage and we may need some heat products for the lower parts of \nthe Grand Canyon as temperatures at Phantom Ranch get close to 110\nthis weekend. \n\nBy Sunday some models are bringing another surge of monsoon\nmoisture into the area. This combined with a east to west steering\nflow will likely lead to another active monsoon pattern for the\nfirst part of next week.",
